 ## Operating characteristics
-* **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources [S1, S2, unofficial][S3, S4].
-* **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources [S1, S2, unofficial][S3, S4].
-* **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources [S1, S2, unofficial][S3, S4].
-* **Strengths:** Supports multiple orders; integrates with high-level frameworks (PyTorch) and low-level libraries (OpenVDB); handles sparse and dense grids [S1, S2, unofficial][S3].
-* **Limitations:** PyTorch's `F.grid_sample` only supports nearest and trilinear natively; higher orders require custom implementations [S1, unofficial]. Out-of-bounds handling must be explicitly managed [S1, unofficial].
-* **When to Use:** Use trilinear for general volume rendering and SDF evaluation. Use nearest for speed-critical applications or when smoothness is not required. Use tricubic for high-quality smoothness where performance allows [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample for dense grids. For sparse grids, $O(\log N)$ or $O(depth)$ depending on tree traversal efficiency [S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory per sample.
+- **Performance:** Trilinear is the fastest and most common. Triquadratic and Tricubic offer smoother gradients but require more memory fetches and arithmetic operations. GPU acceleration (e.g., via B-Spline optimizations) can mitigate the cost of higher orders [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Supports multiple continuity orders; generic interface for various grid types (dense/sparse).
+- **Limitations:** Higher orders increase computational cost. Boundary handling must be explicit.
+- **When to use:** Use Nearest for speed/low quality. Use Trilinear for standard volume rendering. Use Triquadratic/Tricubic for high-quality surface reconstruction or smooth field visualization.
 
 ## Background
-Voxel interpolation is a fundamental technique in volume rendering and scientific visualization [S1, S2, unofficial][S3]. The PyTorch implementation (`F.grid_sample`) is widely used in deep learning for spatial transformer networks and 3D reconstruction [S1, unofficial]. The OpenVDB/NanoVDB implementations are standard in VFX and scientific computing for handling large sparse volumes [S1, unofficial][S3]. The method evolved from basic nearest-neighbor sampling to higher-order polynomial interpolation to improve visual quality and numerical accuracy in SDF representations [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The concept of voxel interpolation is fundamental to volume rendering and scientific visualization. The specific templated class implementation supporting orders 0-3 for NanoVDB/OpenVDB trees originates from the NanoVDB library developed for high-performance volume rendering [S2, unofficial]. The PyTorch integration (`F.grid_sample`) popularized the normalization conventions and differentiable sampling interface for deep learning applications [S1, unofficial]. The factory pattern for creating samplers is part of the NanoVDB math namespace [S3].
 
